Packaged axon-server
====================

Upstream project description: /usr/share/doc/axon-server/README.md
(markdown, same file as the git repository).

The systemd unit runs as Unix user `axon` with
`--config /etc/axon-server/config.toml`.

Web client
----------
axon-server serves its web client itself, on the same address as the
API. After install it is at:

  http://127.0.0.1:8080/

It is reachable from this machine only, because the server listens on
127.0.0.1 ([server] host in config.toml). Nothing is exposed to your
network until you do one of the things under "Access from other
devices" below.

Always open it as 127.0.0.1, not localhost. A browser treats the two
as different sites and keeps a separate sign-in for each, so signing
in at one and coming back at the other shows the sign-in screen again.

To turn the web client off and serve the API alone, add this to
/etc/axon-server/config.toml (under the existing [server] section if
there is one):

  [server]
  web_client = false

then `sudo systemctl restart axon-server`. Remove the line and restart
to turn it back on.

Signing in
----------
First sign-in: the install prints a one-time URL of the form
http://127.0.0.1:8080/bootstrap/<code>. Open it in a browser on this
machine and choose "Create bearer token"; the web client opens already
signed in. If you missed the URL:

  journalctl -u axon-server -e --no-pager | grep -i bootstrap

The URL works once, and only until the first credential exists.

With no browser on this machine, forward the port from a machine that
has one and open the same URL there:

  ssh -L 8080:127.0.0.1:8080 <this machine>

Every later sign-in (another browser, a phone, axon-tui) needs a
token. Mint one and paste it into the client's sign-in screen:

  sudo -u axon axon-server token issue --label laptop
  sudo -u axon axon-server token list
  sudo -u axon axon-server token revoke --label laptop

The secret is printed once. Run these as user `axon`, not as root: see
"Running commands" below.

Access from other devices
-------------------------
The server speaks plain HTTP and refuses to listen on anything but
127.0.0.1 unless told to. To use the web client from a phone or another
computer, put something that terminates TLS in front of it.

Do this AFTER the first sign-in. The one-time first-sign-in page is
limited to requests from this machine, and behind a reverse proxy on
this machine every request comes from this machine.

Tailscale is the least work: no hostname, no certificate, and nothing
exposed to the public internet.

  sudo tailscale serve --bg http://127.0.0.1:8080

For Caddy, nginx, or Apache there is a ready-made site file for each in
/usr/share/doc/axon-server/examples/:

  axon-server.caddy
  axon-server.nginx.conf
  axon-server.apache.conf

Each file starts with the exact commands that enable it. In all three
you replace axon.example.com with your hostname; for nginx and Apache
you also point the two certificate lines at your certificate (Caddy
obtains its own). The package does not install or change any of these
servers: it cannot know your hostname, and enabling a proxy is what
makes this server reachable from outside.

All three files proxy every path to 127.0.0.1:8080, so the web client
and the API stay on one address. That matters: the web client only
talks to the address it was loaded from.

The install output lists the commands for whichever of these it found
on the machine, once. They are not printed again on an upgrade or a
reconfigure; the same commands are at the top of each site file.

Running commands
----------------
`axon-server <command>` finds /etc/axon-server/config.toml without
--config. Run it as user `axon`:

  sudo -u axon axon-server token list

As root the config is found but the database refuses the connection,
because the packaged database URL uses peer authentication for the
`axon` role. Any other user cannot read the config file and is told so.

Local Postgres (default)
------------------------
If a cluster answers on the Unix socket, the first `configure` creates
role `axon`, database `axon`, and `pgcrypto` as the `postgres` superuser,
then runs `axon-server init` as user `axon` with a peer-auth Unix-socket
URL (sqlx form: postgres://axon@%2Fvar%2Frun%2Fpostgresql/axon, no
password). The service is enabled and started.

The generated config.toml comments explain how to point `database.url`
at a remote or passworded server.

`apt purge axon-server` deletes /etc/axon-server (including store_key).
/var/lib/axon-server is left in place; remove it by hand if you want a
clean slate. The Postgres `axon` database is also left in place.

If you install again without that store_key, postinst will refuse to mint
a new key over encrypted account data and print how to restore the old
config or `dropdb axon` and reconfigure.

`apt remove` disables the systemd unit and leaves the config in place.
A later `apt install` will not re-enable it (upgrades preserve the admin's
enable/disable state). Start it with `sudo systemctl enable --now axon-server`.

Data, search, cache, and upload paths are written into config.toml so
`sudo -u axon axon-server …` (for example `search reindex`) uses the same
trees as the service.

Bring your own Postgres
-----------------------
If no local cluster is reachable, the package still installs. After the
database exists (role can use pgcrypto in that database):

  axon-server init --non-interactive \
    --config /etc/axon-server/config.toml \
    --database-url 'postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DB' \
    --no-token
  chown axon:axon /etc/axon-server/config.toml
  chmod 0600 /etc/axon-server/config.toml

init writes only url and store_key. Add these under the existing
[sync] section (do not add a second [sync] table), then [search] and
[media]:

  data_dir = "/var/lib/axon-server/sync"

  [search]
  index_path = "/var/lib/axon-server/search"

  [media]
  cache_dir = "/var/cache/axon-server/media"
  uploads_dir = "/var/lib/axon-server/uploads"

  systemctl enable --now axon-server
